From d60ace648d4cf2dc03a9fff7dcf9fd531e99cc7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 09:20:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] [Core] Release the CPU KV offload region for a process
 checkpoint

The CPU offloading connector keeps its whole region resident and
registered with CUDA after /checkpoint/prepare resets its index, so a
process checkpoint carries --kv-offloading-size bytes of dead data: a
128 GiB region turned an 11.5 GB image into 139 GB and made the restore
slower than a cold boot.

With --checkpoint-release-kv-offload, preparing unregisters the region
on every rank, waits for all ranks, and frees its pages with MADV_REMOVE.
The mappings stay, so no pointer changes. Restoring pre-faults and
registers it again, and fails if the registration does not come back,
since the transfer kernels dereference the host pointers.

Only the shared region of the CPU spec is released. The tiering spec
and SimpleCPUOffloadConnector keep their memory.

Signed-off-by: Will Eaton <weaton@redhat.com>
